00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.309 align:start position:0%
when you are working in chimera you will  usually be visualizing protein  structures  however it is also possible to visualize  structures containing both protein and  nucleic acid components  for example here we have the pdb entry 1  hys a reverse transcriptase enzyme from  hiv  to change the depiction of the  nucleotides that the enzyme is holding  we can go to actions atoms and bonds and  nucleotide objects  under settings we see a panel containing  the various depiction options  for example under the side chain section  we can change the fill slash slab to  ladder  now  the slabs are replaced with rods as  shown on screen  chimera contains many other useful  depiction options under the tools menu  for example under depiction we can color  the structure according to a rainbow  color scheme  i'm using the default color range as  shown here but you can customize your  own colors in the color editor  when we hit apply the structure is  colored according to the range set above  starting with blue at each n terminus  and ending with red at each c terminus  because this protein contains two chains  there are a total of four termini in the  structure two of which are highlighted  on screen  under tools and depiction we can also  color by secondary structure  if we apply the default settings  alpha helices are colored red beta  sheets purple and coils gray  notice that this coloration only applies  to the protein part of the structure not  the nucleic acids  here i have recolored the structure to a  more uniform color scheme to demonstrate  some of the advanced effects that  chimera has to offer  under actions and ribbon we can activate  ribbon edging  this gives the ribbons thicker and more  clearly defined edges  for more effect options we can go to  tools  viewing controls and open up the effects  panel  among the variety of effects shown here  is depth cueing  if we turn off depth cueing the  structure takes on a much flatter  appearance  you can also activate silhouettes which  give the structure a darker outline  you can adjust parameters such as width  and color  for the most clearly defined outlines  set the transparency under a all the way  up to 1  and change the color to black  finally you can turn on shadows for a  more three dimensional appearance  another interesting feature to explore  is the side view  using this feature you can slice through  the structure to determine how much of  it you will see  you can also use chimera to emulate the  distinctive art style that david  goodsale uses in his molecule of the  month column on pdb101  in the examples shown on screen we can  see that this style is characterized by  flat lighting and the use of individual  atoms as opposed to ribbons  to achieve this style the first step is  to go to tools  viewing controls and lighting  here we can change the lighting from its  default setting to ambient  this creates the distinctive flat look  with the lighting configured we can now  go to actions and show the atoms and  bonds  then we must hide the protein ribbons by  setting the selection mode to append  selecting chains a  and b  and under actions choosing ribbon hide  with the selection cleared we can change  the atoms and bonds depiction to sphere  the result bears some resemblance to the  art style of david goodsell  you can take this one step further by  going to actions  color and choosing by element  now each atom in the protein is colored  according to the standard elemental  coloration scheme
 

